<RickenbackerGuideLayout title="Rickenbacker Tuner Dating Guide" metaTitle="Rickenbacker Tuner Dating: Kluson, Grover & Schaller" description="Identify Rickenbacker Kluson, Grover and Schaller tuners. Compare housings, buttons, mounting patterns and model timelines with reference photos." canonical="/rickenbacker-tuner-dating-guide/">
 <section class="rk-hero"><div class="rk-wrap"><p class="rk-kicker">Rickenbacker Identification Guide</p><h1>Rickenbacker Tuner Dating Guide</h1><p class="rk-lede">Identify Kluson, Grover and Schaller tuners by their housings, buttons and mounting patterns. Guitar and bass timelines differ.</p><p class="rk-byline">By <a href="/about-me/">Joe Dampt</a> · Updated October 15, 2026</p><nav class="rk-toc" aria-label="On this page">{topics.map(t=><a href={'#'+t.id}>{t.title}</a>)}</nav><a href="/rickenbacker-serial-numbers/">Back To The Rickenbacker Serial Lookup</a></div></section>
 <section class="rk-section"><div class="rk-wrap rk-article"><div class="rk-note"><strong>Check The Serial And The Model Together</strong><p>Dates below describe common production periods. Parts overlap between models, and reissues or replacements can resemble earlier hardware. Use several details before deciding that a part conflicts with the serial.</p></div><h2 id="jvg-s-22-tuning-machines">Tuning Machines</h2>
      <p>Rickenbacker’s tuner history involves three main suppliers, Grover, Kluson, and Schaller, used at different times on different models. The transitions are model-specific and do not always happen simultaneously across the line. Use the <a href="/rickenbacker-serial-numbers/#decoder">serial lookup</a> to establish a likely period before comparing the installed tuners.</p>
      <h3 id="jvg-s-23-grover-sta-tite-1956-to-1959-1960">Grover Sta-Tite: 1956 To 1959/1960</h3>
      <p>Open-back tuners with cloverleaf buttons, pre-war in design but reliable and affordable. Used on entry-level and some student models: Combo 400 (1956 launch), Combo 450 (1957), and all short-scale Capris (310 to 325) through most of 1959. Full-scale Capri prototypes briefly had them; production standardized on Klusons by end of 1958. The 450 transitioned to Klusons in early 1959; the short-scale Capris held out until the very last guitars made in 1960.</p>
      <h3 id="jvg-s-24-kluson-deluxe-1954-to-1982">Kluson Deluxe: 1954 To 1982</h3>
      <p>The dominant Rickenbacker guitar tuner for nearly three decades. Closed-back, the first of their type when introduced in 1947. Present on Combo 600 and 800 from 1954 onward. White plastic or nickel “bean” buttons in different periods.</p>
      <div class="rk-photo-grid jvg-img2"><RickenbackerPhoto file="images/rickenbacker-serial/rickenbacker-kluson-tuners-scaled.jpg" alt="Closed Kluson tuner housings on a vintage Rickenbacker headstock" caption="Closed Kluson housings on a vintage Rickenbacker. Compare the stamps, buttons and mounting screws with the later example." /><RickenbackerPhoto file="images/rickenbacker-dating/325-vintage-style-tuners.jpg" alt="Closed vintage-style tuner housings on a later Rickenbacker 325" caption="A later 325 carries the same general vintage-style housing and metal-button shape. These tuners show why the housing alone cannot establish a 1950s or 1960s date." /></div>
      <ul>
        <li>Kluson 548 (very large footprint) on 4000 bass, 1957 to 1961</li>
        <li>Kluson 536 (smaller) on 4000/4001 bass from 1963</li>
        <li>Standard Kluson Deluxe on all non-Grover guitars 1960 to 1982</li>
        <li>Kluson closed entirely in late 1981 after the death of John Kluson, forcing Rickenbacker to find alternatives</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="jvg-s-25-grover-slimline-bass-1969-to-1974-and-1982-to-1983">Grover Slimline (Bass): 1969 To 1974 And 1982 To 1983</h3>
      <p>Two-piece pressure-fit sealed back tuners with small footprint. Replaced Kluson on all 4000-series basses in May 1969. <em>Flat cloverleaf keys</em> from 1969 to June/July 1972; <em>“wavy” or S-shaped keys</em> from mid-1972 to July 1974 when Klusons returned. After Kluson’s closure, returned to 4001/4003 basses in mid-to-late 1982 until Schaller BM tuners replaced them in August 1983. The “wavy Grover” on a bass is a reliable indicator for 1972 to 1974.</p>
      <h3 id="jvg-s-26-grover-rotomatic-360-guitar-september-1971-to-early-1985">Grover Rotomatic (360 Guitar): September 1971 To Early 1985</h3>
      <p>Sealed full-size tuners introduced on the 360 six-string in September 1971 as part of the early-1970s 360 upgrades. The Rotomatic’s large bushings required a redesigned headstock, giving rise to the <a href="/rickenbacker-body-binding-and-inlays/#jvg-s-42-headstock-logo-and-spelling">wider “Gumby” headstock</a> on transitional-era 360s. The specific version Rickenbacker used was a two-piece pressure-fit unit, unlike the more common one-piece Rotomatic found on Gibson and other guitars.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Two-piece Rotomatic: September 1971 to early 1984</li>
        <li>One-piece (standard) Rotomatic: early 1984 onward, when the two-piece version appears to have been discontinued</li>
        <li>Also fitted to the 481 and the six-string neck of the 362/12 (both from 1974)</li>
        <li>After Kluson’s closure in 1982, all six-string guitars including those previously on Klusons received Grover Slimline guitar tuners (mid-1982)</li>
        <li>When Slimlines were discontinued (late 1983), all six-string guitars moved to Rotomatics through late 1984</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="jvg-s-27-schaller-m6-mini-m6-to-1985-to-1996">Schaller M6 / Mini M6: 1985 Onward</h3>
      <p>The new “paddle headstock” introduced on all non-reissue six-string guitars in late 1984 was designed to accommodate the Schaller M6’s bushings (same diameter as the Rotomatic). Schallers replaced Grovers in early 1985. Black hardware/black trim (BH/BT) guitars held onto black Rotomatic tuners until Schaller produced black M6 variants, which arrived in approximately March/April 1996, ending Grover’s relationship with Rickenbacker. BH/BT non-reissue six-strings with black Rotomatics date between roughly 1985 and early 1996. The <a href="/rickenbacker-finish-colors/#finish-names">finish-color guide</a> explains why Jetglo describes the body color rather than the tuner finish.</p>
      <h3>Open-Back Bass Tuners</h3><p>Compare bass machines separately from guitar tuners. Check the gear housing, button shape, screw pattern and any maker’s stamp before assigning a type. Compare these details with the <a href="/rickenbacker-body-binding-and-inlays/#jvg-s-28-fingerboard-inlays">fingerboard inlays</a> when checking the model and period.</p><RickenbackerPhoto file="images/rickenbacker-dating/4003-open-back-bass-tuners.jpg" alt="Exposed gears and cloverleaf buttons on Rickenbacker-branded bass tuners" caption="This blue bass has exposed gears, large cloverleaf buttons and Rickenbacker lettering on the plates. The open-back construction differs from the sealed Grover Slimline housings described above." />      <div class="rk-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable Rickenbacker Reference Table" tabindex="0">
